ch of  the characters we've been following for  so long endings that make sense you do  find out a lot more about the gate  builder civilization you get some  insights into their history more about  how they use the proto-molecule and more  about the things that they left behind  it's all presented in an interesting way  where i don't even think you have to  fully understand what's going on with  them to enjoy the way things play out  the unknown aggressors remain somewhat  more mysterious but that's mainly  because they're so alien that they're  kinda beyond our comprehension but you  do learn enough to tie that part of the  story together ty frank has said that he  looks at the nine books as being three  duologies in a trilogy books one and two  go together with the proto-molecule  being discovered and the effect that  that has books three and four are the  ring gates opening and us going through  them and books five and six cover the  war that breaks out as a result of  everything that happened in the first  four books seven through nine are when  things loop back to the protomolecule  aspects since humanity does in fact  start using the ringgate network to  reach the stars the books also do a lot  of genre hopping the authors have said  that book one was a noir detective story  with horror elements book two was a  political thriller book three was a  haunted house story and book four is a  western i would say that books five and  six are a disaster story in a war story  and daniel abraham has referred to the  last three as their epic fantasy trilogy  and he made the distinction that it  wasn't lord of the rings of course it's  all wrapped up in a character-driven  space opera and you might not have even  noticed the genre shifts from watching  the tv show but with the addition of  laconia and their continued meddling  with the proto-molecule there are some  major changes going on that increase the  stakes in the final three volumes and  that does give the trilogy a different  feeling  the big surprise at the beginning of  book 7 is that 30 years have passed  since the transport union was founded in  that time things were pretty quiet  compared to the years leading up to the  war with the free navy earth slowly but  surely started to recover from the  asteroid attacks mars settled into its  diminished role in the system and  several of the new colonies beyond the  ring started to thrive the crew of the  rossi enjoyed uneventful prosperity with  plenty of work available for a gunship  and this quiet stretch in their history  is why the authors decided not to write  books to fill in this time period there  could have been another trilogy in  between but the big story wouldn't have  moved much and i think they made the  right decision to skip it of course alex  is still alive in the books he gets  married and divorced again in this gap  and has a son who's not really in his  life bobby and clarissa become permanent  crew members the latter's health  continues to get worse because of the  black market mods that she got to try to  kill holden but she's still hanging in  there and at the beginning of persepolis  rising holden and naomi are thinking of  retiring i guess i should mention that  aging has changed in the expanse's  timeline if you think about it from the  camp to the beginning of book seven  about 40 years have passed not everyone  is exactly the same age but that does  put everyone around their 70s what you  have to keep in mind is that due to  advances in science and medicine  lifespan has increased and anti-aging  drugs are something that everyone takes  on average on earth people live into  their 120s and a 70 year old resembles a  40 year old in our timeline everyone on  the crew is much older but they're still  not what we would consider aging and  i've seen that some people don't like  this time jump for that reason because  they're older but i think it was  absolutely necessary for the story  things needed to 