ulo  cortizar's point of view and in that you  get an idea of how laconia plans to  shape the events in the final trilogy  they have been actively experimenting  and working with the proto-molecule in  that time and have managed to avoid most  of the pitfalls experienced by their  predecessors  winston duarte holds the position of  absolute military dictator of the entire  system and in the three decades since  the breakaway fleet had passed through  the laconia gate they've built up a  little civilization it's based on a  military culture focused on discipline  and dedication to this cause which  amounts to an updated dream of mars  duarte believes he's the right person to  lead humanity into the next chapter of  its existence and only worries about  what might happen when he can no longer  do that in the prologue you learn that  he has a plan to solve that issue and of  course they are also now ready to return  through the ring and introduce their  ideas to the rest of humanity things  escalate accordingly from there and one  more time the crew of the rossi find  themselves in the middle of things early  in the trilogy you learn that laconia  has achieved their technological  advantage and that the problems that  marco and the free navy cause for the  inner planet set the stage for them to  be unprepared for what they're up  against and i suppose that's what there  is to say about the setup the scale of  the story is expanded and even though  they understand how to avoid having  ships getting eaten by transiting the  rings they happen upon other ways to  agitate the unknown aggressors the crew  do get split up again and not all of  them make it out alive some old  characters return and of course some new  ones emerge as one of the only people  that experienced the bullet on ellis  elvi akoya plays a prominent role and  cara and zan and the strange dogs also  return to play important roles of their  own the gate builder tech they found on  laconia is crucial to their development  but it's not the most interesting thing  that was left behind that distinction  goes to a giant diamond structure the  size of jupiter and the adros system  which is empty otherwise when they  encounter and study that for the first  time they wonder if it might not be a  backup drive containing the memories of  the gate builder hivemind so things get  interesting and weird and as i mentioned  earlier you do learn a lot about these  ancient civilizations the character  stuff with the rossi crew continues to  be top-notch and the ending manages to  do all of them justice i don't know what  else there is to say other than you're  missing out on a lot of great story if  you don't read these books i do think  the best way to go about it is to start  at the beginning and not only because i  think it makes more sense that way i  think in this case the show and books  actually complement each other in a  really great way having the authors  working on both when you look at the  changes it just helps flesh out what  they were going for i'm sure it's  possible to start at book 7 if you've  watched the whole show but you will be a  little disoriented in the beginning and  that will compound the effect of the 30  year time jump doing it that way is  still better than not doing it though  and it's worth mentioning that the audio  books are great in this case because  jefferson maze does a fantastic job  reading them and they're easy to follow  since you're already familiar with most  of the characters from the show and i  think that feels like a good place to  leave things let me know in the comments  if you have any questions about the time  period between the series and if you  have any questions about the final three  books just make sure you mark those with  spoiler tags please like this video if  you enjoyed it please subscribe to my  channel if you haven't already and  thanks for watching i'll talk to you  soon

